[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR BGP configuration on router1. Mechanism: With no eBGP adjacency established, no routes are exchanged between AS1 (195.11.14.0/24) and AS2 (200.1.1.0/24). Router1 therefore has no route to 200.1.1.0/24, so traffic from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) is dropped at router1. Router2, which is configured to peer to 193.10.11.1, continuously retries and reports the session down.

Localization: Router-to-router link is healthy (router1 eth0 193.10.11.1/24 and router2 eth0 193.10.11.2/24 are UP). Router2’s BGP config includes “neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1” and “show bgp summary” shows the neighbor stuck in Active (retrying). On router1, both “show running-config” and “get_bgp_conf” show no BGP neighbor configured at all; “show bgp summary” returns “No BGP neighbors found,” and its RIB contains only connected routes, lacking 200.1.1.0/24.

Root cause: The eBGP neighbor statement toward router2 (193.10.11.2, AS 2) is missing from router1’s BGP configuration, likely due to a misconfiguration or removal.

Corrective fix: On router1, configure the neighbor and activate it under IPv4 unicast: 
router bgp 1
 neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2
 address-family ipv4 unicast
  neighbor 193.10.11.2 activate
  network 195.11.14.0/24
With “no bgp ebgp-requires-policy” already present, no export policy is needed. Commit/reload FRR; BGP should establish and routes for 200.1.1.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24 will be exchanged, restoring pc1↔pc2 connectivity. 
=== END ===